---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import StatusPill from '../../components/StatusPill.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../lib/siteUrl';
---

<Layout
  title="Design Tokens"
  description="Import DTCG 2025.10 token files, bind them to artwork, re-import external edits with a three-way merge, review conflicts token by token, and export without losing descriptions, aliases, or unknown extensions."
>
  <div class="container-custom">
    <main class="tokens-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/features')}>Features</a> / Design Tokens
      </nav>

      <header class="page-hero">
        <StatusPill status="beta" />
        <h1>A token edited in two places should survive both edits.</h1>
        <p>
          Varve reads design tokens as a standards document, not as a bag of
          strings. Import a DTCG 2025.10 file, bind tokens to real properties,
          and when the file changes outside the app, re-import it and get a
          reviewed three-way merge instead of an overwrite.
        </p>
      </header>

      <section class="find-it site-card site-card--surface" aria-labelledby="find-title">
        <p class="eyebrow">Where it lives</p>
        <h2 id="find-title">View, then Variables and Tokens.</h2>
        <p>
          Both halves of the system sit behind one dialog:
          <strong>View &rsaquo; Variables and Tokens&hellip;</strong>, or the
          command palette entry <strong>Open Variables and Tokens</strong>.
          The dialog lists your variables with their authored and resolved
          values, and underneath it the Token Sync center manages sources,
          import previews, updates, and export.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="sync-flow site-card site-card--surface" aria-labelledby="flow-title">
        <div class="flow-intro">
          <p class="eyebrow">One reviewed transaction</p>
          <h2 id="flow-title">Read, review, apply. Nothing happens on the way in.</h2>
          <p>
            A file is parsed, validated, and mapped onto token identities
            before anything touches your document. The preview you approve is
            the exact revision that gets applied, as a single undoable step.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="flow-steps" aria-label="Token sync stages">
          <div><span>01</span><strong>Import</strong><small>Format detection, validation, and diagnostics</small></div>
          <div class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
          <div class="accent"><span>02</span><strong>Bind</strong><small>Tokens back real variables on real properties</small></div>
          <div class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
          <div><span>03</span><strong>Update</strong><small>Base, local, and source merged per token</small></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="capabilities" aria-labelledby="capabilities-title">
        <p class="eyebrow">What it handles</p>
        <h2 id="capabilities-title">Standards behavior, not a partial dialect.</h2>
        <div class="capability-grid">
          <article>
            <h3>DTCG 2025.10 parsing</h3>
            <p>Tokens, groups, <code>$type</code> inheritance, <code>$root</code>, <code>$extends</code>, curly-brace aliases, and JSON Pointer references are recognized by their rules. A type is never guessed from a name or a value's shape.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>First import that actually starts</h3>
            <p>An empty document has no token store yet. Importing creates the source and the store for you, so the first file does not need a setup step before it can be previewed.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>External updates as a three-way merge</h3>
            <p>Re-importing a connected source compares base, local, and source per token. An upstream edit applies, a local edit survives, and an edit on both sides becomes a conflict you decide — never a source-wins overwrite and never a timestamp race.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Conflict review, one token at a time</h3>
            <p>Each conflicting token shows both values and asks for a decision: keep the Varve value or use the source value. Apply stays disabled until every conflict has been decided, so nothing is resolved quietly behind your back.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Deletions are explicit</h3>
            <p>When the source removes a token you still have unchanged, the update removes it and records a tombstone. The backing variable stays as a local literal, so artwork already bound to it keeps rendering instead of breaking.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Metadata survives the round trip</h3>
            <p>Descriptions, deprecation notices, group-level <code>$description</code>, and unknown vendor <code>$extensions</code> are carried through import, edit, and export. Extensions you do not understand are preserved, not interpreted.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>References stay references</h3>
            <p>Aliases export as aliases. A token-level <code>$ref</code> is kept in its authored form rather than being flattened into a value, so downstream tools still see the relationship you wrote.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Resolver documents, one context at a time</h3>
            <p>Resolver files are recognized by their content, not their extension. Modifier contexts are chosen individually and resolved lazily — permutations are never expanded eagerly to guess at what you meant.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Quiet, deterministic export</h3>
            <p>Export writes the same bytes for an unchanged document: no timestamp, no random identifier, no undocumented version field. What you imported and edited is what another tool reads.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="capability-matrix" aria-labelledby="matrix-title">
        <p class="eyebrow">Capability matrix</p>
        <h2 id="matrix-title">What works, and what is still open.</h2>
        <div class="matrix-scroll">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Operation</th>
                <th scope="col">Result</th>
                <th scope="col">Known limit</th>
                <th scope="col">Verified by</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Import a token file</th>
                <td>Validated preview, explicit destination, one undoable transaction</td>
                <td>Unsupported token types are reported, not applied as if accepted</td>
                <td>Unit suite plus a browser end-to-end spec</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Re-import an edited file</th>
                <td>Base/local/source merge with added, updated, deleted, and unchanged counts</td>
                <td>Manual re-import for now — there is no live file watcher yet</td>
                <td>Browser spec asserting the exported bytes after an update</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Resolve a conflict</th>
                <td>Per-token choice between the Varve value and the source value</td>
                <td>Decisions are per token; there is no bulk resolution action</td>
                <td>Component tests covering blocked and resolved states</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Export tokens</th>
                <td>Deterministic canonical JSON with descriptions and extensions preserved</td>
                <td>Group <code>$type</code> and <code>$extends</code> are reported as export notes rather than re-emitted</td>
                <td>Round-trip tests and a browser download assertion</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Vendor formats</th>
                <td>Standards DTCG input and output only</td>
                <td>Tokens Studio and Figma dialects are not claimed as 2025.10 interop</td>
                <td>Not implemented — no claim made</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Automatic source watching</th>
                <td>Re-read on demand through the import workflow</td>
                <td>No background watcher, no Git-backed source, no external write-back</td>
                <td>Not implemented — no claim made</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <aside class="honesty" aria-labelledby="honesty-title">
        <p class="eyebrow">Honest boundaries</p>
        <h2 id="honesty-title">Your document's tokens are not Varve's own theme.</h2>
        <p>
          Imported tokens apply to your artwork, never to the application
          interface: an imported value cannot restyle menus, controls, or
          focus rings, and switching the interface theme cannot rewrite
          authored artwork. The two ownership domains stay separate on
          purpose.
        </p>
        <p>
          The standards surface targets the DTCG 2025.10 Format, Color, and
          Resolver reports — Final Community Group Reports, not W3C
          Recommendations — and the interface says so. Where a native Varve
          capability has no standardized representation, it is kept and
          labeled rather than renamed into a standard it does not implement.
          File watching, Git-backed sources, and vendor adapters are not
          shipped yet, so they are not advertised.
        </p>
        <a href="https://github.com/K-Arthur/varve/blob/master/docs/tokens/dtcgsync-architecture.md">Read the token sync architecture →</a>
      </aside>
    </main>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .tokens-page { max-width: 900px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 3rem 0 5rem; }
  .breadcrumb { color: var(--text-secondary); font-size: .875rem; margin-bottom: 2.5rem; }
  .breadcrumb a { color: var(--text-secondary); }
  .page-hero { max-width: 700px; margin-bottom: 3.5rem; }
  .page-hero h1 { color: var(--text-primary); font: 700 clamp(2.3rem, 6vw, 4rem)/1.05 var(--font-editorial); letter-spacing: -.025em; margin: 1rem 0; }
  .page-hero p { color: var(--text-secondary); font-size: 1.2rem; line-height: 1.7; }
  .eyebrow { color: var(--accent-primary); font-size: var(--type-interface-micro-size); font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .site-card { background: var(--surface-card); border: 1px solid var(--border-default); border-radius: var(--website-radius-card); padding: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 3rem); }
  .find-it { margin-bottom: 2rem; }
  .find-it h2, .flow-intro h2, .honesty h2 { color: var(--text-primary); font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 3vw, 2.3rem); margin: .5rem 0 1rem; }
  .find-it p:not(.eyebrow), .flow-intro p:not(.eyebrow), .honesty p:not(.eyebrow) { color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.75; }
  .flow-steps { align-items: stretch; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr auto 1fr auto 1fr; gap: var(--space-3); margin-top: 2rem; }
  .flow-steps > div:not(.arrow) { border: 1px solid var(--border-default); border-radius: var(--website-radius-surface); display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-2); padding: var(--space-4); }
  .flow-steps .accent { background: var(--surface-accent-soft); border-color: var(--border-accent); }
  .flow-steps span { color: var(--text-tertiary); font-size: .7rem; }
  .flow-steps strong { color: var(--text-primary); }
  .flow-steps small { color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.4; }
  .arrow { align-self: center; color: var(--accent-primary); font-size: 1.25rem; }
  .capabilities { padding: 4rem 0; }
  .capabilities h2, .capability-matrix h2 { color: var(--text-primary); font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 3vw, 2.3rem); margin: .5rem 0 1.5rem; }
  .capability-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: var(--space-4); }
  .capability-grid article { border-top: 2px solid var(--border-accent); padding-top: 1rem; }
  .capability-grid h3 { color: var(--text-primary); margin: 0 0 .5rem; }
  .capability-grid p { color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.65; margin: 0; }
  .capability-grid code, .capability-matrix code { color: var(--text-primary); font-size: .9em; }
  .capability-matrix { padding: 1rem 0 2rem; }
  .matrix-scroll { overflow-x: auto; }
  .matrix-scroll table { border-collapse: collapse; color: var(--text-secondary); min-width: 760px; width: 100%; }
  .matrix-scroll th, .matrix-scroll td { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-default); padding: .85rem .7rem; text-align: left; vertical-align: top; }
  .matrix-scroll thead th { color: var(--text-primary); font-size: .8rem; letter-spacing: .04em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .matrix-scroll tbody th { color: var(--text-primary); font-weight: 600; }
  .honesty { margin-top: 3rem; }
  .honesty p + p { margin-top: 1rem; }
  .honesty a { color: var(--text-link); font-weight: 600; display: inline-block; margin-top: 1.25rem; }
  @media (max-width: 680px) {
    .flow-steps { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .arrow { justify-self: center; transform: rotate(90deg); }
    .capability-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
</style>
